<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_order_by.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-orderby">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-orderby">
  <primary>ORDER BY</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>ORDER BY</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>ORDER BY</refname>
  <refpurpose>sort the working rows</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
ORDER BY <replaceable class="parameter">sort_key</replaceable> [ ASC | DESC ] [ NULLS { FIRST | LAST } ] [, ...]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-orderby-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>ORDER BY</command> sorts the rows currently in the pipeline. It is a clause
   of its own, not only a trailing modifier of a projection, so it sorts anywhere in a
   query and not only at the end. It is written after the clause whose rows it sorts, and
   it applies to that clause's output. That clause is a <xref linkend="cypher-return"/>,
   a <xref linkend="cypher-with"/> or a <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Rows are compared by the first sort key. Ties go to the next key, and so on. Each key
   sorts ascending unless <literal>DESC</literal> is written, and
   <literal>ASC</literal> is the explicit default. Null values sort last under
   <literal>ASC</literal> and first under <literal>DESC</literal>, unless
   <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> or <literal>NULLS LAST</literal> says otherwise.
  </para>

  <para>
   A sort key is an expression, not a column number. <literal>ORDER BY 2</literal> sorts
   by the constant 2, which orders nothing. Always name the key.
  </para>

  <para>
   A sort key may name an item of the projection it follows, either as the whole key or
   inside a larger expression, so <literal>RETURN m.title AS title ORDER BY
   toUpper(title)</literal> resolves. It may equally read a variable the projection does
   not output, such as <literal>ORDER BY m.year</literal> under a
   <command>RETURN</command> of the title alone. Variables in scope are looked up first,
   so a name that means something to the pattern keeps that meaning.
  </para>

  <para>
   A key that computes with a volatile item is refused with
   <literal>ORDER BY cannot compute with</literal>, naming the item. The key is a second
   occurrence of that item's expression, so a volatile item would be evaluated once for
   the value returned and once for the key, and the rows would be ordered by values other
   than the ones they show. Naming such an item as the whole key is still allowed,
   because that sorts on the item itself, evaluated once.
  </para>

  <para>
   After <literal>RETURN DISTINCT</literal>, every sort key has to appear in the
   projection.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>ORDER BY</command> cannot open a query and cannot end one. A sort produces no
   result on its own, so the query still has to finish with <command>RETURN</command>,
   <command>FINISH</command> or a write clause.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-orderby-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">sort_key</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An expression over the bindings in scope. After a <command>RETURN</command> or a
      <command>WITH</command> this includes that clause's own item names. It may also be
      an expression that was not projected at all.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ASC</literal></term>
    <term><literal>DESC</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Sort the key ascending or descending. <literal>ASC</literal> is the default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>NULLS FIRST</literal></term>
    <term><literal>NULLS LAST</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Where null values go. The default is <literal>NULLS LAST</literal> for an ascending
      key and <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> for a descending one.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-orderby-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Sort by a projected column:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.year &gt; 2015
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year
ORDER BY year;

            film            | year
----------------------------+------
 "Blade Runner 2049"        | 2017
 "The Matrix Resurrections" | 2021
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Several keys sort in the order they are written, each in its own direction. Here
   <literal>genre</literal> is ascending and <literal>year</literal> descending within
   each genre:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.genre AS genre, m.year AS year, m.title AS film
ORDER BY genre, year DESC, film
LIMIT 5;

  genre   | year |            film
----------+------+----------------------------
 "Action" | 2021 | "The Matrix Resurrections"
 "Action" | 2012 | "The Avengers"
 "Action" | 2003 | "The Matrix Reloaded"
 "Action" | 2003 | "The Matrix Revolutions"
 "Action" | 1999 | "The Matrix"
(5 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> leaves nulls behind. The same query without
   <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> puts them last, because the key is ascending;
   <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> asks for the other placement without reversing the
   sort:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE p.born &gt; 1974
OPTIONAL MATCH (p)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN p.name AS person, m.title AS directed
ORDER BY directed NULLS FIRST;

      person       | directed
-------------------+-----------
 "Ryan Gosling"    |
 "Sam Worthington" |
 "Neill Blomkamp"  | "Elysium"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Sorting in the middle of a query, with a <xref linkend="cypher-limit"/>, narrows what
   the rest of the query sees. The second <command>MATCH</command> runs against two rows,
   not eighteen:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
ORDER BY m.year DESC LIMIT 2
MATCH (p:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m)
RETURN m.title AS film, p.name AS director
ORDER BY film;

            film            |      director
----------------------------+--------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"        | "Denis Villeneuve"
 "The Matrix Resurrections" | "Lana Wachowski"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-orderby-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>ORDER BY</command> cannot open a query, so a statement that starts with it is
   a syntax error:
<programlisting>
ORDER BY 1;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "ORDER"
LINE 1: ORDER BY 1;
        ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Ending a query on it raises
   <literal>Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Sorting a property sorts a jsonb value, and jsonb orders by type before value: a
   number sorts above every string, and both above null. A key whose stored values have
   mixed types therefore groups by type first. Cast the key when the types are mixed and
   one ordering across them is wanted.
  </para>

  <para>
   A returned value sorts by the value itself rather than by the jsonb form the
   projection hands back, whenever the value has a type with its own ordering. Rows and
   ordering are the same either way for every ordered type except a float that is not
   finite, which used to sort among strings. The gain is that an index on the value can
   serve the ordering, so a sort key that repeats an indexed expression reads the index
   instead of sorting every row.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-orderby-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>ORDER BY</command> implements the sort specification list of ISO/IEC
   39075:2024, clause 16.17, as a clause of the order by and page statement of clause
   14.9. Complex expressions in sort keys (feature GQ14), sort keys naming a variable the
   projection did not output (features GQ16 and GA07) and aggregates in sort keys
   (feature GF20) are all supported. The standard leaves the default null ordering to the
   implementation; AgensGraph uses last for an ascending key and first for a descending
   one. Two deviations remain. The standard accepts <literal>ASCENDING</literal> and
   <literal>DESCENDING</literal> alongside the short spellings, and AgensGraph accepts
   only <literal>ASC</literal> and <literal>DESC</literal>. And the standard's sort key is
   a value expression with no ordinal form, so <literal>ORDER BY 2</literal> is the
   constant 2 and reorders nothing rather than naming the second column as SQL would.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher writes <command>ORDER BY</command> only as a trailing modifier of
   <command>RETURN</command> or <command>WITH</command>. Both of those forms are accepted
   here unchanged. The standalone clause, which sorts after a
   <command>MATCH</command> or anywhere else in a query, and the explicit
   <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal> and <literal>NULLS LAST</literal> placements are
   additions.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

